# Commands and results

- `./gradlew.bat :ddd-domain-event-jpa:test --tests com.only4.cap4k.ddd.domain.event.JpaEventScheduleServiceTest --no-daemon`: passed 11 tests, including asynchronous Integration Event lease renewal, retryable completion transitions, synchronous Domain Event completion, and token-bound payload-load failure.
- `./gradlew.bat :cap4k-ddd-domain-event-jpa-starter:test --tests com.only4.cap4k.ddd.starter.event.JpaEventCoordinatorCompositionIntegrationTest --no-daemon`: passed 2 real H2/JPA composition tests using the production provider, publisher, coordinator, substrate, and repository.
- `./gradlew.bat :ddd-core:test :ddd-domain-event-jpa:test :cap4k-ddd-domain-event-jpa-starter:test :cap4k-ddd-integration-event-http-starter:test :cap4k-ddd-integration-event-rabbitmq-starter:test :cap4k-ddd-integration-event-rocketmq-starter:test --no-daemon`: passed the complete focused matrix. This includes atomic claim/token/lease/retry fixtures, core publisher/supervisor behavior, JPA production composition, and HTTP/RabbitMQ/RocketMQ starter boundaries.
- `git diff --check`: passed with no whitespace errors; Git reported only repository line-ending conversion advisories.
- Static `rg` audit: the only production `eventPublisher.publish(...)` call is in `JpaEventScheduleService` after claim; Event repository saves remain enrollment-only; Event delivery transitions use the JPA substrate CAS methods; deleted Event state APIs were not reintroduced.
- `comet native check runtime-reliable-event-state`: passed. Receipt: `runtime/evidence/check-receipts/0f9a58a3ab39c6248cc7ac1b80793ddae04a8982ad69f3d8d0e78abe381010d6.json`.

# Skipped checks

- No real HTTP, RabbitMQ, or RocketMQ broker was started. Outbound provider callback semantics are covered with focused publisher and coordinator tests.
- No process-kill fault injection was run after an external side effect and before acknowledgement.
- No dedicated timed test suppresses the after-commit wake and waits for the scheduled recovery tick; due-record claim and retry recovery are verified directly.
- Scoped `queries.askAsync*` and `capabilities.callAsync*` join/failure behavior was not re-tested because it belongs to the existing application execution scope.
- Derived outbound Event release/rollback across a parent Domain Event handler transaction has no focused end-to-end fixture in this slice.

# Spec consistency

- Persisted Domain Events and outbound Integration Events share one private JPA claim/lease/token state machine.
- Claim precedes payload dispatch or provider handoff. Acknowledgement and failure remain token and live-lease bound.
- First delivery attempt is durable attempt `1`; persistence initialization remains attempt `0`.
- After-commit callbacks only wake the coordinator. They do not dispatch or mutate delivery state directly.
- Domain Event completion remains synchronous at the publisher boundary; Integration Event provider callbacks may complete asynchronously while the coordinator renews the lease.
- No transport envelope, inbound acknowledgement, manual redrive, retention, generic task API, or legacy Event compatibility surface was added.

# Known limitations and risks

- Build scope is intentionally partial only because four retired files are deleted and cannot be declared as physical artifacts: `EventProperties.kt`, `DefaultEventInterceptorManager.kt`, `ReliableDomainEventExecutionContextTest.kt`, and `DefaultEventInterceptorManagerTest.kt`. Comet recorded the explicit partial allowance.
- Real persistence composition uses H2 in MySQL compatibility mode; production-database lock timing and broker-specific behavior remain deployment verification concerns.
- The runtime is explicitly at-least-once. A side effect completed before lease loss may be delivered again.
- The focused suite does not replace the broader application-execution tests for scoped async joins or derived outbound transaction behavior.

# Conclusion

Pass. The implemented reliable Event state machine matches the confirmed change contract for ownership, lease renewal, token fencing, completion, retry, safe failure facts, after-commit wake-up, public scheduling boundaries, and starter composition. The skipped checks and partial-scope deletions are documented and do not introduce a competing ownership path.
